  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nA warm and dry weekend is expected across the region. This is thanks \nto subtle upper-level ridging building in from the west. \nTemperatures are forecast to rebound back into the mid 80s to low \n90s for a lot of locations which will continue through early next \nweek. For context, these temperatures are 10-15 degrees above normal \nfor this time of year. Luckily, the well above normal temperatures \nand dry weather look to come to an end for some by early next week. \nLong-range guidance has an upper-level system approaching from the \nWest Coast, while Tropical Storm Simon moves near the Gulf of \nCalifornia. A rich plume of moisture from Simon combined with \nsurface troughing across the Rockies will supply low (10-30%) rain \nchances late Monday into Tuesday for far west Texas and southeast \nNew Mexico. Ensemble and deterministic guidance are beginning to \nhighlight the remnants of Tropical Storm Simon being absorbed \ninto the upper-level system by Monday evening. Strong \nsouthwesterly flow aloft supplies further low rain chances \n(20-40%) across much of the region Tuesday through Wednesday \nmorning. Ensembles depict PWATs reaching above the 90th to near \nmaximum climatological percentile indicating the threat of heavy \nrainfall. Deep southwesterly flow aloft also creates a favorable \npattern for a few severe storms to develop. Strong winds across \nthe Guadalupe/Delaware Mountains will be another potential hazard \nto monitor. There still remains uncertainty in the timing, \npositioning, and progression of the upper-level storm system \nduring this timeframe. Therefore, details on potential weather \nimpacts for the forecast area should be ironed out as we get \ncloser in time. Stay tuned! Confidence is highest that \ntemperatures will begin cooling down by mid to late next week.\n\nLamberson",
